[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Request Mapping: Mapping URLs to Controller Methods in Spring Boot"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"In Spring Boot, request mapping is the core mechanism for directing incoming HTTP requests to the appropriate controller methods. This tutorial provides a comprehensive guide on how to effectively map URLs to controller methods using various annotations and techniques."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"Understanding Request Mapping Annotations"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Spring provides several annotations for request mapping, each serving a specific purpose. The most fundamental is `@RequestMapping`, but more specialized annotations like `@GetMapping`, `@PostMapping`, `@PutMapping`, `@DeleteMapping`, and `@PatchMapping` offer a more concise and semantic approach."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"@RequestMapping"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `@RequestMapping` annotation is the most versatile and can handle any HTTP method. It can be used at both the class and method levels. When used at the class level, it defines a base URL for all handler methods within that controller."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"@Controller
@RequestMapping("/products")
public class ProductController {

    @RequestMapping(value = "/list", method = RequestMethod.GET)
    public String listProducts(Model model) {
        // Logic to retrieve products
        return "products/list";
    }

    @RequestMapping(value = "/add", method = RequestMethod.POST)
    public String addProduct(@ModelAttribute Product product) {
        // Logic to add a product
        return "redirect:/products/list";
    }
}"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the example above, `@RequestMapping("/products")` at the class level means that all methods within `ProductController` will handle requests starting with `/products`. The `listProducts` method handles GET requests to `/products/list`, while the `addProduct` method handles POST requests to `/products/add`."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"@GetMapping, @PostMapping, @PutMapping, @DeleteMapping, @PatchMapping"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"These specialized annotations provide a more concise way to map requests based on the HTTP method. They are essentially shorthand versions of `@RequestMapping` with the `method` attribute already specified."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"@Controller
@RequestMapping("/products")
public class ProductController {

    @GetMapping("/list")
    public String listProducts(Model model) {
        // Logic to retrieve products
        return "products/list";
    }

    @PostMapping("/add")
    public String addProduct(@ModelAttribute Product product) {
        // Logic to add a product
        return "redirect:/products/list";
    }

    @GetMapping("/{productId}")
    public String viewProduct(@PathVariable Long productId, Model model) {
        // Logic to view a specific product
        return "products/view";
    }

    @PutMapping("/{productId}")
    public String updateProduct(@PathVariable Long productId, @ModelAttribute Product product) {
        // Logic to update a product
        return "redirect:/products/view";
    }

    @DeleteMapping("/{productId}")
    public String deleteProduct(@PathVariable Long productId) {
        // Logic to delete a product
        return "redirect:/products/list";
    }

    @PatchMapping("/{productId}")
    public String patchProduct(@PathVariable Long productId, @RequestBody Map<String, Object> updates) {
        // Logic to partially update a product
    	return "redirect:/products/view";
    }
}"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"This example demonstrates the use of `@GetMapping`, `@PostMapping`, `@PutMapping`, `@DeleteMapping`, and `@PatchMapping` to handle different HTTP methods for various product-related operations. Notice the use of `@PathVariable` to extract values from the URL."},{"order":13,"type":"heading","text":"Path Variables and Request Parameters"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Path Variables (@PathVariable)"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Path variables allow you to extract values directly from the URL path. They are defined using curly braces `{}` in the `@RequestMapping` value and are accessed in the method signature using the `@PathVariable` annotation."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"@GetMapping("/users/{userId}/posts/{postId}")
public String getPost(@PathVariable Long userId, @PathVariable Long postId, Model model) {
    // Logic to retrieve the post
    return "posts/view";
}"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, `userId` and `postId` are extracted from the URL and passed as arguments to the `getPost` method."},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Request Parameters (@RequestParam)"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"Request parameters are key-value pairs appended to the URL after a question mark (`?`). They are accessed in the method signature using the `@RequestParam` annotation."},{"order":20,"type":"code","text":"@GetMapping("/search")
public String search(@RequestParam String keyword, @RequestParam(defaultValue = "1") int page, Model model) {
    // Logic to perform the search
    return "search/results";
}"},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, `keyword` and `page` are extracted from the URL parameters. The `defaultValue` attribute in `@RequestParam` provides a default value if the parameter is not present in the request."},{"order":22,"type":"heading","text":"Consuming and Producing Media Types"},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can specify the media types that a controller method can consume (accept) and produce (return) using the `consumes` and `produces` attributes of the `@RequestMapping` annotation (or its variants)."},{"order":24,"type":"code","text":"@PostMapping(value = "/upload", consumes = MediaType.MULTIPART_FORM_DATA_VALUE)
public String uploadFile(@RequestParam("file") MultipartFile file) {
    // Logic to handle file upload
    return "upload/success";
}

@GetMapping(value = "/report", produces = MediaType.APPLICATION_PDF_VALUE)
public ResponseEntity<byte[]> generateReport() throws IOException {
    // Logic to generate PDF report
    return ResponseEntity.ok()
            .header(HttpHeaders.CONTENT_DISPOSITION, "attachment; filename=report.pdf")
            .body(pdfBytes);
}"},{"order":25,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `uploadFile` method only handles requests with `Content-Type: multipart/form-data`. The `generateReport` method produces a PDF document with `Content-Type: application/pdf`."},{"order":26,"type":"heading","text":"Advanced Mapping Techniques"},{"order":27,"type":"subheading","text":"Custom Request Mapping Conditions"},{"order":28,"type":"paragraph","text":"For more complex scenarios, you can create custom request mapping conditions by implementing the `RequestCondition` interface. This allows you to define your own logic for matching requests based on headers, parameters, or other request attributes."},{"order":29,"type":"subheading","text":"Using Regular Expressions in Path Variables"},{"order":30,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can use regular expressions within path variables to further refine the matching of URLs. For example, you can restrict a path variable to only accept numeric values."},{"order":31,"type":"code","text":"@GetMapping("/products/{productId:[0-9]+}")
public String getProduct(@PathVariable String productId, Model model) {
    // Logic to retrieve the product
    return "products/view";
}"},{"order":32,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, the `productId` path variable will only match if it consists of one or more digits."},{"order":33,"type":"heading","text":"Best Practices"},{"order":34,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use specialized annotations (`@GetMapping`, `@PostMapping`, etc.) for better readability and maintainability."},{"order":35,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Keep your controller methods focused and responsible for a single task."},{"order":36,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use meaningful and consistent URL patterns."},{"order":37,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Handle exceptions gracefully and return appropriate HTTP status codes."},{"order":38,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Consider using a REST client like Postman or Insomnia to test your API endpoints."},{"order":39,"type":"heading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":40,"type":"paragraph","text":"Request mapping is a fundamental aspect of Spring Boot web development. By understanding the various annotations and techniques discussed in this tutorial, you can effectively map URLs to controller methods and build robust and well-structured web applications."}]
